      <!-- Four views. The default one is 840 x 800 (the board 640 x 640 at (100, 80), the HUD in the margins), the view named Portrait 720 x 960 (the board at (40, 144), the material above and below it)
           and the view named Wide 1120 x 800 (the same board and margin, 280 more units on the right for the move panel).
           The board is 640 x 640 and a square 80 units in all four, so a piece, a drag and a label keep their numbers. `when` is tested on the OUTPUT in pixels: with the board 640 units in both views
           it is 640 * min(W / viewW, H / viewH) pixels, which is bigger in Portrait exactly while W / H is under 840 / 960 = 0.875 (a width-bound landscape view against a height-bound portrait one),
           so the view with the bigger board on the screen is picked; a square window keeps the default view. maxAspect holds up to and including its number, so the tie (0.875) is Portrait.
           Wide is picked from the aspect 1120 / 800 = 1.4 up (minAspect holds from its number): there the height already limits the default view, so the scale is outputHeight / 800 in both and the board,
           the HUD and every font keep their size; the free letterbox beside the view is what the panel takes. Below 1.4 the width limits the default view, and widening it would shrink everything, so
           those windows keep the view they have. A view is never picked for being wider or higher, only where that costs no scale.
           Tall (720 x 1204) is Portrait made higher for the narrow phones held upright (9:16, 9:19.5), from the aspect 0.59 down: 720 / 1204 = 0.598, so the width limits it there as it
           limits Portrait and the scale is outputWidth / 720 in both; its extra height under the board is what the move panel takes, and no more: a higher view would leave a 9:16 window too thin a bar above it, and a
           notch would push the HUD's top row onto the upper row of the material. It comes before Portrait because the first variant that holds wins.
           ChessBoardComponent follows the active view (its portraitOrigin* and portraitSquareSize are the board of Portrait and of Tall, which keeps every place of Portrait); a canvas lays out
           per view with a variant `orientation:portrait` (a canvas variant is conditioned on the bounds of its own canvas, here the view in units: 720 x 960 and 720 x 1204 are portrait,
           840 x 800 and 1120 x 800 are not). -->
      <viewVariants>
        <view name="Tall" when="maxAspect:0.59" width="720" height="1204" />
        <view name="Portrait" when="maxAspect:0.875" width="720" height="960" />
        <view name="Wide" when="minAspect:1.4" width="1120" height="800" />
      </viewVariants>
